From bbcb82ef3ac361f6a6be7b1b3fbf641ecd7c11c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 08:41:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] rdnsmapd: Flow Explorer destination names from a passive DNS
 map

The Connections page printed every destination as a bare address. rdnsmapd
learns names at DNS time instead of resolving from the page: an AF_PACKET
socket with a 16-instruction classic BPF filter (udp src port 53) blocks in
recv, and every A record in a reply's answer section is stored under the
question's name, which follows the CNAME chain for free. Zero idle CPU, no
timer, no sweep.

The table is a 131,136-byte tmpfs file (/tmp/reaper/dnsmap, 2048 x 64-byte
slots, open addressing, probe window 8, earliest-expiry replacement, TTL
clamped to 1-24 h) that the daemon and httpd both mmap. The layout is the
contract, in shared/reaper_dnsmap.h; httpd probes it in place per flow and
extends an entry while it is on screen. No publish step, no parse step.

Names on/off (nvram rconn_names, default 1): off stops the daemon and removes
the table. Switching it back on runs a one-shot backfill: conntrack's remote
ends (private, CGNAT and already-named addresses skipped, capped at 512) get
one PTR query each to the local resolver at about 50 per second, stored as
reverse names that a later forward reply replaces. The page keeps a name while
its address is listed and forgets it when no flow to it remains, labels the
router's own bridge addresses "This router", and shows reverse names muted.

The Advanced detail panel now follows the reader down the list: inside the
shell frame nothing scrolls, so sticky never engaged; the panel is placed from
the visible slice the shell publishes (--rv-top/--rv-h). The two-column layout
gains a compact band and stacks below 1110 px instead of overflowing.

Mixed hunks in this commit: services.c also carries the one-time DTIM 1
default (once per box, marker /jffs/reaper/.dtim1) and drops a stale
wlcsm_bindfix value from nvram; rc.h their externs; the router Makefile also
stops installing the toolchain's x86-64 libexpat.so into /lib of the image.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
